*(
    title: Reduction Formulae
    description: An integral defined in terms of a smaller copy of itself, evaluated by descending to a base case.
    tags: integration, reduction formulae, recurrence, calculus, extension 2, stub
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Integrating the fourth power of sine by parts does not finish the job; it leaves the second power behind. That looks like a failure until the same calculation is done with a general exponent, at which point the leftover is the point: it relates one integral to its smaller neighbour.

    Written out here: deriving a reduction formula by parts, the base case that terminates the descent, applying the formula repeatedly to evaluate a definite integral, and the definite-integral versions in which the boundary term vanishes and the formula simplifies.
}
